\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
GreenREFORM-EU 3 Architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Martin Kirk Bonde
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
August 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this document,
 I outline a new version of the GreenREFORM model system.
 The document is extremely terse —
 written to be readable by experts,
 without fluff or trivial details that make it harder to grasp the big picture.
 We assume close familiarity with optimizing representative agent models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We omit time indices,
 except where there is a lead or lag.
 We reuse variable names,
 where indices in subscripts can be used to distinguish.
 Aggregates are equal to the sum of the same variables with an additional index,
 e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=\sum_{i}Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 —
 we leave out the simple aggregates for brevity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prices are implicitly aggregated as 
\begin_inset Formula $P\cdot X=\sum_{x}P_{x}X_{x}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Model layers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model is built in three layers,
 with each layer depending on the previous layers existing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The core accounting layer is useful for testing if data is internally consistent and for basic input output analysis.
 This layer should be free of assumptions about economic behavior as far as possible.
 Its fixed shares are default closures that behavioral modules can replace.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The macroeconomic core provides a New Keynesian model of demand for private consumption,
 exports,
 intermediate inputs in production,
 and capital investments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Green transition core adds <Berg>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Satellite modules are sub-models that do not define variables used in other modules.
 Instead,
 connections between modules should use core models as intermediaries in order to minimize linkages.
 Extensions attach to the core in two ways:
 they can endogenize core parameters,
 such as the share parameters,
 or they can provide equations for friction variables that core modules define as hooks and that default to zero.
\end_layout

