\begin_layout Section
Sector accounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our sector accounts match the national accounts,
 except we lump non-profit institutions serving households together with households.
 Each sector also corresponds to one or more agents in the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{sectors}=\left\{ \text{Households},\text{Government},\text{Financial corporations},\text{Non-financial corporations},\text{Rest of the world}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting keeps track of the financial assets and liabilities of all sectors and all transfers between them.
 Non-financial assets are not part of the sector accounts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A key feature of sector accounting is that all net variables sum to zero over sectors.
 E.g.
 total net debt is zero,
 as any debt liability has a corresponding debt asset.
 Net assets,
 financial income,
 financial transfers between sectors,
 and revaluations all sum to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sector modules vs sector accounts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each sector,
 corresponding to one or more agents,
 has a dedicated module.
 These dedicated modules contain sector-specific specifications of the net financial transactions of the sectors —
 e.g.
 net income and expenditures in the budget constraint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are reconciled with a condensed budget constraint in the sector accounts file,
 which simply states that the period to period change in net assets equals the net financial transactions of the sector plus any revaluation of assets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each sector modules must specify how net savings are split between assets and liabilities,
 and between different financial instruments (debt vs equity).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Splitting net positions into assets and liabilities is practical,
 as the alternative is to introduce an additional asset type to distinguish between domestic and foreign equity.
 With explicit liabilities,
 the equity liability of the rest-of-the-world equals the total foreign equity owned by domestic sectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Input output
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The input output system keeps track of all flows of goods and services.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Indices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\text{industries}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=\text{products}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u=\text{uses}=i\cup\left\{ C,G,X,K\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is government consumption,
 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is consumption by households and non-profits,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 are exports,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 are investments in fixed assets or inventories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Industry split (supply table)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default assumption is fixed industry shares for each product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{p,i}=\mu_{p,i}Y_{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Endogenous industry shares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
CET split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
<Berg>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Energy markets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
<Berg>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Product split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We default to fixed shares of products for each direct use:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,u}=\mu_{p,u}D_{u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The shares can be endogenized by other modules to allow substitution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Country of origin split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We split all direct product demand by country of origin:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,u,o}=\mu_{p,u,o}D_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
o=\left\{ \text{domestic},\text{import}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using an index for origin makes it easy to split imports later,
 for example into Euro-zone vs rest-of-the-world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Import-substitution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To introduce import-substitution with a satellite model,
 endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

.
 Substitution by product,
 rather than also by use,
 might be enough.
 Demand responds to relative purchaser prices (see Trade margins):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,u,o}=\hat{\mu}_{p,u,o}\left(\frac{P_{p,u,o}^{D}}{P_{p,u}^{D}}\right)^{-E_{p}}D_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Production (cost minimization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the output to be produced,
 the production module calculates a marginal cost of production and the demand for each input factor into production.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Production function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume a nested CES production function with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{factors of production}=k\cup r\cup l
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 are types of capital,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 are types of intermediate inputs,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 are types of labor (with one type being perfectly fine).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\text{factors of production}\cup\text{nests}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Demand for the factor or nest 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{f}$
\end_inset

 in a specific industry (we omit the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 index here) is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{f}=\mu_{f}X_{N}\left(\frac{P_{f}}{P_{N}}\right)^{-E_{N}}\forall f\in N
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where N is a production nest and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{f}$
\end_inset

 is the usercost of the production factor or nest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The usercost of a nest is given by the budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{N}X_{N}=\sum_{f\in N}P_{f}X_{f}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercost of factors of production can either be simple spot prices,
 or come from complicated usercost expressions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We implement the nesting structure very compactly,
 using a single variable and demand equation for all factors of production,
 letting a mapping handle the nesting structure.
 The main upside is that this enables us to vary the nesting structure depending on the industry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Capital adjustment costs and other frictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Various frictions should be added to the simple usercost expressions,
 e.g.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
E.g.
 the usercost of capital is something like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{k}^{f}=P_{k}^{I}+\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}-\frac{1-\delta_{k,t+1}}{1+r_{t+1}}\left(P_{k,t+1}^{I}-\frac{\partial T_{t+1}^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}\right)+\text{marginal capital adjustment cost}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The friction itself should be detailed in a satellite model,
 not in the production module.
 Friction variables are hooks that default to zero when no satellite model provides them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 marginal taxes (here 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial T^{\text{Corp}}}{\partial K_{k}}$
\end_inset

) are defined elsewhere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Output and marginal cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Output is the top nest less friction losses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y=X_{N=\text{TopNest}}-\text{friction losses}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Friction losses can for example be foregone output due to capital adjustment costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal cost of production is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MC\cdot Y=P_{N=\text{TopNest}}Y+\text{production taxes}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Frictions do not enter this equation directly.
 They affect the price through the usercosts inside the nest:
 the marginal capital adjustment cost enters the usercost of capital,
 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{N=\text{TopNest}}$
\end_inset

,
 while the average (total) capital adjustment cost is subtracted from output at the top.
 
\begin_inset Formula $MC$
\end_inset

 is therefore the cost of one more unit of output,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $MC\cdot Y$
\end_inset

 is not total cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Behavior vs accounting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercosts are behavior,
 not accounting.
 The usercost of capital contains the hurdle rate of the firm.
 Marginal cost and the markup derive from this subjective usercost,
 so they are model constructs,
 not neutral accounting.
 The sector account of firms records actual payments instead:
 revenue 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}^{Y}Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 less labor compensation,
 intermediate inputs,
 investments,
 and total (not marginal) taxes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the input-output system capital investments enter as a single column.
 In the production module,
 or in a satellite module,
 we split capital investments into multiple types of fixed assets,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{K}=\sum_{k}I_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{k}=\sum_{i}I_{k,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,K}$
\end_inset

 by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,K}=\sum_{k}I_{p,k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each type of capital,
 we assume fixed product shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{p,k}=\mu_{p,k}I_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function subject to capital accumulation laws of motion.
 Changes in inventory are lumped with equipment capital investments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of intermediate inputs (energy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can split use in production into different types of intermediate inputs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p,i}=\sum_{r}\mu_{p,r,i}R_{r,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where for example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r=\left\{ \text{transport energy},\text{heating},\text{processing energy},\text{other intermediates}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{r,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that assuming identical product shares for energy inputs across industries is not needed and does not simplify much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Markups in basic prices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We replace our standard model of monopolistic competition with fixed markups by industry with a model of fixed markups by 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{product}\times\text{use}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
I am on the fence about whether this is a good idea.
 Please comment!
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our fundamental assumption is that the marginal cost of producing a product is independent of how the product is used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal cost of producing a product is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{MC}_{p}Y_{p}=\sum_{i}\text{MC}_{i}Y_{p,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The basic price of a product is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{p,u}^{Y}=\theta_{p,u}\text{MC}_{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 is the markup parameter.
 It results from monopolistic competition between a continuum of otherwise identical firms;
 the firm-level demand elasticity behind the markup is disconnected from the macro substitution elasticities.
 It can be endogenized to model price rigidity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Basic prices by product and industry are given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{p}^{Y}Y_{p}=\sum_{u}P_{p,u}^{Y}Y_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{i}^{Y}Y_{i}=\sum_{p}P_{p}^{Y}Y_{p,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is also what determines profits by industry (
\begin_inset Formula $\left[P_{i}^{Y}-\text{\emph{MC}}_{i}\right]Y_{i}$
\end_inset

 at the margin).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Trade and transport margins
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Trade and transport margins are the wholesale,
 retail,
 and transport services needed to deliver a product to its user.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $s=\left\{ \text{wholesale},\text{retail},\text{transport}\right\} \subset p$
\end_inset

 index margin service products.
 Each user has a fixed-share margin-service bundle.
 We use the standard origin shares for each margin service:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{u}^{M}=\sum_{s,o}\mu_{s,u}^{M}\mu_{s,u,o}P_{s,u,o}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 be direct use.
 Each unit needs 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 units of the margin service bundle:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
M_{u}=\sum_{p}m_{p,u}D_{p,u},\qquad M_{s,u,o}=\mu_{s,u}^{M}\mu_{s,u,o}M_{u},\qquad M_{p,u,o}=0\quad\forall p\notin s.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 be the domestic basic price or import border price,
 with
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,u,\text{domestic}}=P_{p,u}^{Y}$
\end_inset

.
 The purchaser price of direct use is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{p,u,o}^{D}=\left(P_{p,u,o}+t_{p,u,o}+m_{p,u}P_{u}^{M}\right)\left(1+t_{p,u,o}^{\text{VAT}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the price that enters into any substitution decision and the direct-demand aggregate:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{u}^{D}D_{u}=\sum_{p,o}P_{p,u,o}^{D}D_{p,u,o}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The product balance is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{p,u}=D_{p,u,\text{domestic}}+M_{p,u,\text{domestic}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Margin demand enters the product balance but not
\begin_inset Formula $D_{u}$
\end_inset

.
 Its value is already in the purchaser price of direct demand,
 so do not add
\begin_inset Formula $P_{u}^{M}M_{u}$
\end_inset

 to the user budget.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household module determines total demand for private consumption (consumption savings decision).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Consumption savings decision
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default model is a household maximizing CRRA utility with habits,
 a hand-to-mouth consumption share of income,
 and utility of wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Private consumption types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We place all products with direct household demand in a CES nest tree.
 Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c=\text{products}\cup\text{consumption nests}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{c}=\mu_{c}C_{N}\left(\frac{P_{c}}{P_{N}}\right)^{-E_{N}}\quad\forall c\in N,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{N}C_{N}=\sum_{c\in N}P_{c}C_{c}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{N}=0$
\end_inset

,
 the children have fixed quantity shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Exports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model export demand directly for domestic production of each product,
 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p,X}$
\end_inset

,
 using the final purchaser price 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{p,X,\text{domestic}}^{D}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imports for re-export,
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p,X,\text{import}}$
\end_inset

,
 are completely exogenous.
 To accommodate this,
 the import share of exports,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,X,o}$
\end_inset

,
 is endogenized.
 Alternatively,
 we can split the use column 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 into two:
 direct exports vs re-exports.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Government
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Including basic demand for public consumption and government transfers to other sectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is no fiscal closure by default:
 government debt drifts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Taxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tax accounting and marginal tax rates (derivatives of total revenues wrt.
 to various tax bases).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally,
 other modules should only use aggregate marginal tax rates and aggregate tax revenues.
 E.g.,
 a household budget contains a total taxes paid variable,
 while a labor market decision equation uses only a marginal tax on labor income.
 The break down of marginal taxes into income brackets etc.
 is kept in the taxes module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Labor market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The default model is a forward-looking Phillips curve with exogenous labor supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sector accounting and endogenous revaluation rate of equity through a discounted cash flow valuation of the firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One module covers both financial and non-financial corporations.
 The non-financial sector sums profits over industries;
 the sector accounting system redistributes them to owners.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Rest of the world
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Closure of the sector accounting system and financial markets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Foreign prices and fixed interest rate from ECB belongs here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Aggregates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Accounting aggregates not covered by other parts of the model.
 E.g.
 GDP.
\end_layout

